<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ditadura Nacional</h1><p class="st-meta">1926 CE – 1933 CE · capital Lisbon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The military dictatorship that ended Portugal's First Republic and brought António de Oliveira Salazar to power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 28 May 1926 a military coup launched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-gomes-da-costa">Manuel Gomes da Costa</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-portuguese-republic">First Portuguese Republic</a>. Within weeks Gomes da Costa was pushed aside by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-carmona">Óscar Carmona</a>, who became president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The military regime dissolved parliament, introduced censorship and repressed several revolts. Unable to solve the financial crisis, it appointed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> economics professor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a> as finance minister in 1928, with control over all spending. He balanced the budget and steadily accumulated power, becoming prime minister in 1932.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A new constitution, approved by plebiscite in 1933, transformed the military dictatorship into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">Salazar</a>'s civilian, corporatist <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo (Portugal)</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-carmona">Carmona</a> remaining president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ditadura-nacional.jpg" alt="Ditadura Nacional" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1926 CE – 1933 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lisbon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Gomes da Costa, Óscar Carmona, António de Oliveira Salazar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>National Dictatorship (Portugal)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=ditadura-nacional" aria-label="Ditadura Nacional on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4453 789 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ditadura Nacional lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4773 1045L4773 1045M4773 1045L4773 1044M4773 1045L4773 1045"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1045h0M4773 1044h0M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lisbon. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=ditadura-nacional">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
